course: dictionary
lesson: 2495
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning. Dear is a good example: it can mean loved, it can mean costing a lot, and it can be the polite first word of a letter. You need the context to know which one is meant.
explain: In «Thank you so much, dear Ratty!» dear is a kind word for a friend, not a price. In «Spices were dear in medieval Europe», dear is about cost: the spices cost a lot. The words around the word tell you which meaning to use.
explain: Check by swapping in your meaning. If «costing a lot» fits the sentence, you have it right. In «Dear Grandma», dear just opens a letter politely, and it has nothing to do with money or feelings.
fact: Dear means loved or very much liked, as in «my dear friend».
fact: Dear is the polite word at the start of a letter: «Dear Grandma,» with a comma after the name.
fact: In shops, mostly in the UK, dear means costing a lot, and dearer means more expensive.
fact: «Oh dear!» shows you are sorry or worried, for example when something goes wrong.
fact: Dear can also be a kind word for someone you love, as in «Thank you, dear».
exercise: «Thank you so much, dear Ratty!» What does dear show here? | *Love and fondness for a friend | Bad weather | A high price | Sorrow
exercise: «Spices were dear in medieval Europe.» What does dear mean here? | Worried | Polite, at the start of a letter | Loved very much | *Costing a lot
exercise: Mia starts a letter with ___ Grandma, ... Which word goes in the gap? | Thank | Oh | Dearer | *Dear
test: What does «Oh dear!» show? | Anger | *Sorry or worried | Joy at a party | Hunger
test: «Trouble there can make petrol dearer far away.» What does dearer mean? | Closer | Happier | *More expensive | Kinder
exam: In «Dear Grandma, thank you for the cake», what job does dear do? | It means the cake was costly | It shows she is sad | Shows the price of the cake | *It is the polite word that opens a letter
